{"birds": [{"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 56, "first": "10:36 AM", "last": "4:12 PM", "ts": 1795793800, "mins": [636, 646, 651, 651, 652, 652, 653, 654, 655, 656, 656, 657, 660, 660, 662, 662, 685, 709, 709, 710, 718, 782, 782, 782, 787, 788, 794, 796, 797, 797, 798, 814, 814, 817, 841, 890, 891, 892, 893, 893, 920, 924, 932, 932, 965, 965, 966, 966, 966, 968, 968, 969, 969, 971, 971, 972], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png?v=1793046043", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 3, "first": "11:03 AM", "last": "11:04 AM", "ts": 1795795412, "mins": [663, 663, 664], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png?v=1793046044", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "v": 1793046044, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Dark-eyed Junco", "sci": "Junco hyemalis", "n": 4, "first": "11:04 AM", "last": "2:24 PM", "ts": 1795795453, "mins": [664, 664, 670, 864], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Often called “snowbirds” because they show up as winter arrives. The white outer tail feathers flash as they flit away.", "vox": "sounds/voice/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113009", "snd": "sounds/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113154", "sndby": "Phoenix Birder, xeno-canto XC563632, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "junco", "cm": 15.5, "img": "cutouts/junco.png?v=1793991313", "w": 534, "h": 447, "len": 625.0, "v": 1793991313, "beak": [0.022, 0.14, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 27, "b": "D4AAAD/AAAA/4AAA//AAAP/wAAA//AAAH/4AAD//AAA//4AAP//AAD//4AA///AAH//4wB///cAP//+AB///AAf//wAB//+AAP//wAA//8AAH+PgAD8B8AH+AHgD/gA8D+AAHg/gAA8AAAAH"}}, {"name": "White-breasted Nuthatch", "sci": "Sitta carolinensis", "n": 6, "first": "11:05 AM", "last": "4:03 PM", "ts": 1795795511, "mins": [665, 670, 715, 796, 962, 963], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "Nuthatches climb down tree trunks head first. The name comes from “nut-hack”: they wedge a seed into bark and hack it open.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113050", "snd": "sounds/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113095", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC14860, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "nuthatch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/nuthatch.png?v=1793031430", "w": 532, "h": 327, "len": 372.0, "v": 1793031430, "beak": [0.79, 0.42, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 20, "b": "fgAAAH8AAAB/AAAAP4AAAD+AAAAfwAAAH/4gAB///gAf///AB///gAf//gAD//wAA//4AAP/8AAB//AAAf/AAAD+AAAA/gAAAP4AAAB/AAA="}}, {"name": "American Crow", "sci": "Corvus brachyrhynchos", "n": 9, "first": "11:12 AM", "last": "3:08 PM", "ts": 1795795920, "mins": [672, 672, 672, 763, 766, 767, 770, 795, 908], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Crows remember individual human faces for years, and they seem to pass the grudge along to other crows.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793112990", "snd": "sounds/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793113335", "sndby": "David Vander Pluym, xeno-canto XC289011, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "crow", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/crow.png?v=1793031428", "w": 553, "h": 364, "len": 568.0, "v": 1793031428, "beak": [0.94, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 21, "b": "AAAHwAAAD/gAAB/+AAB//gAD/+AAD//AAB//wAB//4AB//+AB///AAf//wAP//4AH//4AH//8AD//8AA/x+AAPgPwAbwF/6/f////3///7Bhw5AA"}}, {"name": "Downy Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryobates pubescens", "n": 42, "first": "11:42 AM", "last": "3:59 PM", "ts": 1795797721, "mins": [702, 703, 725, 726, 726, 726, 727, 727, 749, 749, 750, 752, 753, 753, 754, 754, 755, 795, 795, 796, 796, 797, 798, 860, 860, 861, 861, 862, 862, 863, 863, 863, 864, 864, 941, 957, 957, 958, 958, 959, 959, 959], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "North America’s smallest woodpecker. It is small enough to forage on thin weed stalks and twigs that bigger woodpeckers can’t cling to.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793112995", "snd": "sounds/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793113092", "sndby": "Thomas G. Graves, xeno-canto XC89855, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "downy", "cm": 16.0, "img": "cutouts/downy.png?v=1793045350", "w": 486, "h": 800, "len": 707.0, "v": 1793045350, "beak": [0.59, 0.1, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 53, "b": "AAAAAP/4AAD//AAA//wAAP/8IAD//DwA//wf4H/8D/x//Af+f/wH/3/8A/9//AP/f/wD/3/8B/9//A//f/wf/n/8P/x//H/8f/7//H/+//x////8f////D////x////8f////H////h////4f///+D////A////wP///4D///+A////gP///wD///8A///+AP///gD///wA///4AP//+AD///AA///wAP//sAD//4AA//8AAP//AAD//wAA//4AAP/+AAD//gAA//4AAP/8AAD//AAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 12, "first": "11:42 AM", "last": "1:27 PM", "ts": 1795797774, "mins": [702, 703, 708, 721, 792, 792, 801, 801, 804, 805, 806, 807], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png?v=1793046043", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/gwAD/wYAA/8GAAf/PAAH/7wAB//8AA//+AAf//4AH/+/AD//vwA//38Af/98AP//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4APg/wADgH8ABwA+AA4APgAeAH4AHAB8ADgA/AAwAeAAMAPAAAAHwAAADwAAAA8AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Red-bellied Woodpecker", "sci": "Melanerpes carolinus", "n": 3, "first": "12:22 PM", "last": "1:54 PM", "ts": 1795800159, "mins": [742, 834, 834], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "The “red belly” is only a faint pink blush you will rarely see. The red cap is the giveaway. Its barbed tongue can reach about two inches past the bill tip.", "vox": "sounds/voice/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113012", "snd": "sounds/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113332", "sndby": "Bruce Lagerquist, xeno-canto XC713255, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redbellied", "cm": 24.0, "img": "cutouts/redbellied.png?v=1793031431", "w": 400, "h": 800, "len": 516.0, "v": 1793031431, "beak": [0.52, 0.23, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 64, "b": ""}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 1, "first": "1:13 PM", "last": "1:13 PM", "ts": 1795803231, "mins": [793], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png?v=1793031427", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "v": 1793031427, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPgAAAD+AAAA/4AAAH+AAAD/8AAB//AAA//gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAB//4AAf/+AAf//gAH//zAD//5wA//94Af+OcAP/8eAD9/fgA/l/gAPgHwAHgD4AHwA4AB8AeAA+AGAAfADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 7, "first": "1:57 PM", "last": "4:56 PM", "ts": 1795805833, "mins": [837, 909, 946, 947, 972, 973, 1016], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png?v=1793990894", "w": 484, "h": 472, "len": 557.0, "v": 1793990894, "beak": [0.021, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "D4AAAH/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAA/4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAH/8AAB//gAAf/+AAH//wAA//+AAH//wAB//+AAP//gAA//8AAH//gAA//+AAHZ/wAB2P8AA5h+AAcwP4f/uA+P//+Dj////4////+P////gD///4AAD/+AAAAPg=="}}, {"name": "Cedar Waxwing", "sci": "Bombycilla cedrorum", "n": 1, "first": "3:13 PM", "last": "3:13 PM", "ts": 1795810401, "mins": [913], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "The red tips on its wing feathers look like drops of sealing wax, which gave waxwings their name. They sometimes pass berries beak to beak along a row of perched birds.", "vox": "sounds/voice/bombycilla-cedrorum.mp3?v=1793112972", "snd": "sounds/bombycilla-cedrorum.mp3?v=1793113168", "sndby": "Rory Nefdt, xeno-canto XC1142128, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "waxwing", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/waxwing.png?v=1793045350", "w": 800, "h": 738, "len": 708.0, "v": 1793045350, "beak": [0.61, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 30, "b": "AAAADgAAAAwAAAAMAA8ADwAfwB8AD/AfAB/x2AAf4fgAP8H4AH+B/AD/xPwA/8X4Af/HgAP/hwAD/4YAB/+OAA//nAAP/zAAD/5wAB/9wAA//4AAP/8AAH/eAAA8+gAAeHAAAHDgAADj4AAAw4AAAEcAAAAGAAAA"}}, {"name": "Rock Pigeon", "sci": "Columba livia", "n": 1, "first": "4:13 PM", "last": "4:13 PM", "ts": 1795813992, "mins": [973], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Pigeons have been domesticated for thousands of years, and homing pigeons can find their way back across hundreds of miles.", "vox": "sounds/voice/columba-livia.mp3?v=1793986166", "snd": "sounds/columba-livia.mp3?v=1793113284", "sndby": "Paul Marvin, xeno-canto XC485507, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "rockpigeon", "cm": 32.0, "img": "cutouts/rockpigeon.png?v=1793987498", "w": 800, "h": 544, "len": 872.0, "v": 1793987498, "beak": [0.994, 0.201, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AAAAPgAAAH4AAAB+AAAA/wAAB/0AAD/8AAD//AAB//wAB//8AA///AAf//wAP//8A///+AP///gB///wA///wA/h/wA+AB4A+AD/4OAA/+AAAP/gAAD/4A=="}}], "also": [], "date": "Friday, November 27", "where": "", "updated": "5:00 PM", "sun": null, "wx": {"t": 45, "kind": "cloudy", "word": "Cloudy", "hi": 52, "lo": 38}, "code": "10a791c1d1", "listen": {"state": "on", "since": null, "control": false}}